<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="sinh-cosh-complete">
    <title>Hyperbolic Sine and Cosine (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Hyperbolic trig functions arise in solutions to differential equations. We'll work to understand
                        what these functions are both analytically and geometrically.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="4in" >
            <title>Analytic</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Recall, for a function <m>f(x)</m>, 
                        <md>
                            f(x) = f_{\text{even}}(x) + f_{\text{odd}}(x)
                        </md>
                        
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Letting <m>f(x) = e^x</m>, we get 
                        <md>
                            <mrow> e^x \amp= \frac{e^x + e^{-x}}{2} + \frac{e^x - e^{-x}}{2}</mrow>
                            <mrow> \amp:= \cosh(x) + \sinh(x) </mrow>
                        </md>
                    We can use the definition of hyperbolic cosine and sine to define other hyperbolic trig functions. 
                    For example, <m>\tanh(x) := \frac{\sinh(x)}{\cosh(x)} = \frac{e^x-e^{-x}}{e^x+e^{-x}}</m>.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
    
    </page>
    <page>

            <exercise>
                <title>Geometric</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                    Why <q>hyperbolic</q>?
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task workspace="2in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            The unit hyperbola: 
                            <md>
                                x^2-y^2 =1
                            </md>
                            
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            You can verify that <m>\cosh^2(x)- \sinh^2(x)= 1</m>, which is analogous to sine and cosine
                            satisfying the equation of the unit circle: <m>\cos^2(x) + \sin^2(x) = 1</m>.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>

                <task workspace="2in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            The unit hyperbola and areas:
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            See the <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/eljvt2craz">Hyperbolic Trig Desmos graphs</url> 
                            to visualize how the values of <m>\cosh</m> and <m>\sinh</m> come from <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> coordinates
                            of points on the unit hyperbola.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
                
                
            </exercise>
        </page>

        <page>
            <exercise workspace="8in">
            <title>Euler's Formula and Hyperbolic Functions</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Recall Euler's formula:
                    <md>
                        e^{ix} = \cos(x) + i \sin(x)
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    This should remind us a lot of <m>e^x = \cosh(x) + \sinh(x)</m>. If we substitute the imaginary 
                    number <m>ix</m>, we get 
                    <md>
                        <mrow> e^{ix} \amp= \cosh(ix) +\sinh(ix)</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp= \frac{e^{ix}+e^{-ix}}{2} + \frac{e^{ix}- e^{-ix}}{2}</mrow>
                    </md>,
                    where the first term is a purely real (why?) and the second term is purely imaginary (why?). Equating 
                    this with Euler's formula and matching the real parts and imaginary parts, we see that
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\cos(x) \amp= \cosh(ix) </mrow>
                        <mrow> \sin(x) \amp= \frac{\sinh(ix)}{i} </mrow>
                    </md>,
                    which provides a connection between <m>\cos/\cosh</m> and <m>\sin/\sinh</m> in complex space.
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
                
            </exercise>
        </page>

       
    </worksheet>
